{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/service at exact clean head 7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff, succeeding ae477605. Like the cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact filed alongside it, this exists because the merge preflight refused and was right to: the head changes internal/service/server_test.go, a maintained path my three artifacts did not cover. Companion to 806c884d at cmd/gs, 780daff3 at internal/statusview, 43e1f8a8 at docs and bdbc5a1b at cmd/gitseq-mcp; all five must be co-signed together.\n\nWHAT CHANGED HERE: one test file. No resident source moves. The addition is the HTTP half of the guard the requester's ruling 56565ba9 required — the resident's /v0/artifact-query is the other surface that was silently honouring undeclared selectors, because it decodes the same struct the MCP adapter posts to it. The test asserts that a request carrying the CLI-only selectors is refused rather than served, so the endpoint's accepted contract is pinned to what it declares.\n\nWHY BOTH GUARDS AND NOT JUST ONE. MCP and HTTP failed the same way for the same reason but are reached differently, and a single test would leave the other path free to drift. Codex's verdict reports running the real mutation and seeing both fail: the MCP guard with retired selecting zero instead of one live row, and this one because state=all returned 200 where the contract refuses it. Two independent alarms on one shared cause is the right shape when the cause is a struct that two surfaces share.\n\nSECURITY, and this is the path where it matters most. internal/service is the resident's HTTP boundary — reachable by everything inside the trust boundary, and the surface whose accepted inputs are hardest to walk back once agents write against them. The change here narrows what it accepts: before this lane it would have honoured two selectors it never declared, and now it refuses them and a test says so. Loopback resolution, deadlines, response bounds and frontier checks are untouched. Nothing is signed, admitted or authorised differently.\n\nMY MISS, RECORDED HERE TOO SO IT IS NOT ONLY IN THE OTHER ARTIFACT. Before requesting review I checked that no competing live candidate existed at the three paths I had published. I never asked whether the head touched other maintained paths at all, and it touched two. An approval that does not cover a retired path would let a merge withdraw another actor's pointer on a verdict that never looked at it, which is exactly what the preflight refused to do.\n\nGATES at this exact head, run by me on a clean tree: go vet exit 0; go build exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok including internal/service; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0 with no data race; gofmt empty; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff","path":"internal/service"}}